<section class="prose" aria-labelledby="research-focus-title">
  <p class="eyebrow">Research focus</p>
  <h2 id="research-focus-title">From ARKIVO to Community Nodes</h2>
  <p>
    The project is centred on ARKIVO.ART, a preservation platform developed through
    Daniel Filipe Farinha's doctoral research. ARKIVO provides the immediate case
    study because it already has a defined preservation mission and a working
    technical base.
  </p>
  <p>
    The post-doc investigates how SEDA nodes could extend this preservation work
    beyond a primarily centralised platform. Candidate node roles include pinning
    artefacts, mirroring snapshots, verifying metadata, preserving dependencies,
    and documenting operator policies for cultural-preservation infrastructure.
  </p>
  <p>
    The broader research question is social as well as technical: how can
    decentralised cultural infrastructure remain sustainable, trustworthy, and
    useful for artists, collectors, researchers, and institutions?
  </p>
  <p>
    The work also aims to act as a case study for the decentralisation of the
    Teia platform on Tezos, using ARKIVO as a bounded preservation testbed from
    which transferable infrastructure patterns can be studied.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="cards" aria-labelledby="themes-title">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Themes</p>
    <h2 id="themes-title">What the Project Studies</h2>
  </div>
  <article class="card">
    <h3>Preservation beyond the chain</h3>
    <p>
      Blockchain records alone do not preserve rendering environments, APIs, IPFS
      availability, metadata, gateways, or artist-maintained code.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article class="card">
    <h3>Volunteer infrastructure</h3>
    <p>
      Community nodes turn preservation into shared stewardship, while raising
      questions about reliability, verification, accountability, and operator burden.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article class="card">
    <h3>Teia Platform</h3>
    <p>
      ARKIVO is the bounded testbed. The project asks which practices could inform
      decentralised infrastructure for Teia.
    </p>
